Subject: Pace cycle collection by whether the last collection reclaimed anything

What this does
  Replaces the fixed heap-growth rearm at both collection sites with a
  productivity-gated one: a collection that reclaimed almost nothing widens the
  interval that follows it, and one that reclaimed something restores it.

Why we need it
  The rearm is a function of heap growth alone and never asks whether the
  collection it just paid for reclaimed anything. Everything acyclic is freed at its
  last decrement without the collector, so a collection finds nothing at all unless
  the program built a cycle.

What it adds
  A collection is unproductive when it reclaimed less than 1/64 of the heap, or
  less than 256 KB absolutely. Each consecutive unproductive collection doubles the
  growth step, bounded as a percentage of the live heap (400%) and never tighter
  than the unmodified 1.5x, so a large heap cannot balloon and a small one may back
  off a long way; one productive collection resets the streak. Delaying a
  collection that would have freed nothing cannot retain anything it would have
  freed, and the bound releases garbage produced afterwards at the next productive
  collection. JS_GetGCPacingCap and JS_SetGCPacingCap expose the streak cap,
  default 8; 0 restores the unmodified rule exactly. The policy is one helper
  called from both the inline trigger and the deferred-collection path. No opcode
  is added or retired and BC_VERSION is unchanged.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

